{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P17792","uniProtkbId":"VIRB2_AGRFC","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"1990-08-01","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-11-04","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1990-08-01","entryVersion":135,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":5.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Agrobacterium fabrum (strain C58 / ATCC 33970)","commonName":"Agrobacterium tumefaciens (strain C58)","taxonId":176299,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Alphaproteobacteria","Hyphomicrobiales","Rhizobiaceae","Rhizobium/Agrobacterium group","Agrobacterium","Agrobacterium tumefaciens complex"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Type IV secretion system pilin protein VirB2"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"17975085"}],"value":"Major T-pilus component VirB2"}}],"flag":"Precursor"},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"2194232"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"2233252"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"2370849"}],"value":"virB2"},"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"Atu6168"}],"orfNames":[{"value":"AGR_pTi_4"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10428832"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11741876"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9573157"}],"value":"Component of the VirB/VirD4 type IV secretion system (T4SS), which translocates transfer DNA (T-DNA) and virulence effector proteins into plant host cells, leading to crown gall disease (PubMed:9573157). The VirB2 subunit is the pilin, the major structural subunit of the T-pilus (PubMed:10428832, PubMed:11741876, PubMed:9573157). It is essential for virulence (PubMed:11741876)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9573157"}],"value":"Produces higher amounts of exocellular VirB2 at 19 than at 28 degrees Celsius (PubMed:9573157). Processed VirB2 accumulates in the cell in similar amounts irrespective of the growth temperature (19 versus 28 degrees Celsius), suggesting that the temperature effect is on the exportation of VirB2 (PubMed:9573157)"}],"commentType":"ACTIVITY REGULATION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"O50328"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15901731"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36513067"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36750723"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36870333"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9573157"}],"value":"VirB2 is the major subunit of the pilus, a component of the VirB/VirD4 T4SS that extends from the core secretion machinery to the outside of the cell (PubMed:36513067, PubMed:36750723, PubMed:36870333). It polymerizes (PubMed:36513067, PubMed:36750723, PubMed:36870333, PubMed:9573157). Five VirB6 subunits may form a platform and contain a binding or recruitment site for five pilin subunits, which are then levered up to the assembly site, while five more pilin subunits are recruited to the vacated binding/recruitment sites (By similarity). As layers of pentameric pilins are added, the pilus extends and displaces the VirB5 pilus minor subunits upwards and through the outer membrane to the extracellular milieu (By similarity). Forms long thin extracellular filaments of variable lengths (PubMed:9573157). VirB2 is arranged in five-start helical assemblies (PubMed:36513067, PubMed:36870333). Each pilin subunit forms extensive interactions with 6 other subunits (PubMed:36513067). VirB2 interacts with VirB5, a minor T-pilus component that localizes at the tip of the pilus (PubMed:15901731). The T-pilus also contains phospholipids, with a 1:1 stoichiometric assembly of VirB2 pilin proteins and phospholipid (PubMed:36513067, PubMed:36750723, PubMed:36870333)"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"O50328"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"P09776"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"17975085"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"22027007"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"24971727"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8405938"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8824616"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9573157"}],"value":"Prior to being assembled into a pilus, the pilin subunits are located in the inner membrane, forming a pool for subsequent use in building the pilus (By similarity). Pilus assembly requires the pilin subunits to be extracted from the membrane through the concerted action of the VirB4 and VirB11 ATPases (By similarity). Upon receipt of an unknown signal, the pilin subunits are extracted from the inner membrane into the periplasmic assembly site located in the interacting surfaces of the VirB6 and VirB5 proteins (By similarity). Both propilin and processed pilin are detected inside cells, but only processed pilin is detected extracellularly (PubMed:24971727). Export of the processed VirB2 requires other virB genes (PubMed:9573157). Localizes along the entire length of the T-pilus (PubMed:17975085). Localizes to multiple foci around the bacterial cell perimeter during lateral attachment to plant cells (PubMed:22027007). Inside cells, is associated primarily with the inner membrane but is also associated, albeit at much lower concentrations, with the outer membrane (PubMed:8405938, PubMed:8824616)"}]},"subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8405938"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8824616"}],"value":"Cell inner membrane","id":"SL-0037"},"topology":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255"}],"value":"Multi-pass membrane protein","id":"SL-9909"}},{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11741876"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"24971727"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9573157"}],"value":"Secreted","id":"SL-0243"}},{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"17975085"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36513067"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36750723"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36870333"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9573157"}],"value":"Fimbrium","id":"SL-0113"}}]},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10428832"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11741876"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36513067"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36750723"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36870333"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8405938"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8824616"}],"value":"Produced as a propilin (PubMed:10428832, PubMed:11741876, PubMed:8405938, PubMed:8824616). The propilin is first processed by the removal of an unusually long signal sequence, most likely by the general signal peptidase I LepB, to generate a cleaved VirB2 protein (PubMed:10428832, PubMed:11741876, PubMed:8405938, PubMed:8824616). This cleaved VirB2 protein may be further cyclized via formation of a covalent bond between the N-terminal Gln and the C-terminal Gly residues, leading to the formation of a cyclic mature T-pilin subunit (PubMed:10428832, PubMed:11741876). However, the cryo-EM structures do not provide evidence for the presence of a cyclic peptide (PubMed:36513067, PubMed:36750723, PubMed:36870333). The cyclic VirB2 may thus represent an intermediate form that is later linearized or, as VirB2 appears to have multiple roles during mating-pair formation, a different functional form (PubMed:36870333)"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"commentType":"MASS SPECTROMETRY","method":"MALDI","molWeight":7184.3,"molWeightError":0.0,"note":"Cyclized form.","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11741876"}]},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Belongs to the TrbC/VirB2 family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Signal","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":47,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10428832"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"8405938"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"8824616"}]},{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":48,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":121,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Type IV secretion system pilin protein VirB2","featureId":"PRO_0000022660"},{"type":"Topological domain","location":{"start":{"value":48,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":71,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Periplasmic","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"P09776"}]},{"type":"Transmembrane","location":{"start":{"value":72,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":89,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Helical","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"P09776"}]},{"type":"Topological domain","location":{"start":{"value":90,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":94,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Cytoplasmic","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"P09776"}]},{"type":"Transmembrane","location":{"start":{"value":95,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":113,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Helical","evidences":[{"evidenceCod